{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790306059,"nonce":"Y7t8ClM2iziUIih6F80agMx/HD4=","title":"Lua: the os and io conformance cases have no Windows expectation","message":"`stdlib/os.lua` and `stdlib/files.lua` are pinned from lua5.4 on a POSIX host and are marked `unix` in `crates/zyntax_lua/conformance/KNOWN_FAILURES`, so the Windows job skips them and reports the skip. What they pin is the host's answer, not the language's:\n\n- `os.lua`: the reference's strftime conversions are per platform (loslib.c accepts `L_STRFTIMEWIN` on Windows, which refuses `%e`, `%D`, `%F` and `%Ey`; zylua accepts the C99 set everywhere), `%c`, `%x` and `%p` are the C runtime's words, `os.getenv(\"HOME\")` is usually unset there, `os.tmpname()` is not rooted at `/`, and `os.execute(\"true\")` names a command cmd.exe lacks.\n- `files.lua`: `io.popen(\"echo piped; exit 3\")` and `io.popen(\"cat \u003e ...\", \"w\")` are sh; cmd.exe also writes CRLF, which the reference's text-mode `_popen` turns into LF and zylua's pipe reader does not.\n\nCovering Windows means a Windows expectation for each, pinned from lua5.4 built for Windows (a second pin beside `.expected`, chosen by the harness), plus whatever the pinning shows zylua getting wrong there, the popen line ending first. Needs a Windows machine.","files":null}]}